        {
          "step_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s03",
          "type": "explainer",
          "concept_ids": [
            "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Chart Type Matching",
          "title": "Pie charts, scatterplots, and a comparison trap",
          "lede": "Part-to-whole framing calls for a pie chart — but comparing pie slices is harder than it looks.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s03.b1",
              "role": "practical_context",
              "text": "A pie chart expresses a part-to-whole relationship in the data. It is best used for a general sense of how parts relate to the whole, or to highlight that one segment is notably larger or smaller than the others — and the pie must depict a meaningful whole (values that sum to 100%). If the point is 'Lagos accounts for most of our total delivery volume,' a pie chart of city order shares fits that framing in a way a bar chart of averages would not.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb3"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f4"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s03.b2",
              "role": "practical_context",
              "text": "A scatterplot is often used to understand data rather than to communicate with it, though it can still be designed to convey findings to an audience. It is not the default choice for a non-technical stakeholder presentation, but if the distance-versus-delivery-time relationship needs to go in front of the operations manager, a scatterplot can work.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb4"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f6"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s03.b3",
              "role": "misconception",
              "text": "It can look like comparing pie slices is just as easy as comparing bar lengths — but it is not. A bar chart's aligned, common baseline makes length differences easy to judge; two pie slices of similar size are harder to rank correctly by eye. When precise comparison between categories matters, a bar chart is the stronger choice.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb5"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#m1"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "reward": {
            "gems": 5
          },
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb3",
