        {
          "step_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s06",
          "type": "explainer",
          "concept_ids": [
            "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Simplifying Charts",
          "title": "Remove the clutter your audience doesn't need",
          "lede": "A chart built for your own exploration can carry gridlines, a border, and precise decimal labels. The operations manager probably doesn't need any of that to get the point.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s06.b1",
              "role": "intro",
              "text": "A bar chart of city delivery times built during your own exploration can carry gridlines, a border, and precise decimal labels that help you double-check the numbers. The operations manager probably doesn't need any of that to get the point. Each element you remove reduces the visual effort required to read the chart.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#tb1"
                ],
                "fact_ids": []
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s06.b2",
              "role": "mechanism",
              "text": "Gridlines can be deleted from a chart; if precise values matter to the audience, label the data point directly instead of relying on gridlines. A chart's border can be removed, since it is not adding informative value — white space can differentiate parts of a visual better than a border.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#tb2"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#f1",
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#f2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s06.b3",
              "role": "mechanism",
              "text": "Data labels should be used sparingly, only where exact values are important; otherwise remove them and use the axis instead. Unnecessary trailing zeroes can be dropped — '22.00' becomes '22 min'. Diagonally rotated axis text is slower to read; city names like Lagos, Abuja, and Kano are short enough to render horizontally.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#tb3"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#f3",
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#f5",
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#f6"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
