            "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Recap",
          "title": "What you've learned: matching and simplifying charts",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s11.b1",
              "role": "takeaway",
              "text": "Chart choice is driven by the point you are making, not the data alone. Bar charts compare categories; line graphs show trends; pie charts express part-to-whole relationships. Once the right chart is chosen, remove every element that adds visual effort without adding information — border, gridlines, trailing zeros, rotated labels, unnecessary data labels. Reducing clutter lets the audience use their attention to decide what actions are warranted, rather than working out how to read the graph.",
