      "remediation_steps": [
        {
          "step_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.r01",
          "type": "remediation",
          "concept_ids": [
            "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Let's revisit",
          "title": "Scatterplots: exploratory vs. communicative — a closer look",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.r01.b1",
              "role": "mechanism",
              "text": "A scatterplot is often used to understand data rather than to communicate with it. When you plot delivery distance against delivery time for yourself, you are exploring a relationship. That same chart can be designed to convey a finding to an audience — but it is not the default choice for a non-technical stakeholder presentation, because the audience must first understand what a scatterplot shows before they can read the point. If the relationship needs to go in front of the operations manager, consider whether a simpler chart can make the same point.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb4"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f6"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb4"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f6"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "step_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.r02",
          "type": "remediation",
          "concept_ids": [
            "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Let's revisit",
          "title": "Matching chart type to stakeholder point — a contrast",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.r02.b1",
              "role": "mechanism",
              "text": "Bar charts compare values across categories — use one when the point is 'Lagos takes longer than Abuja.' Line graphs show how a value changes over time — use one when the point is 'Lagos delivery time has risen each week.' Pie charts express part-to-whole relationships — use one when the point is 'Lagos accounts for most of our total order volume' and the values sum to 100% (Lagos 57.1%, Abuja 28.6%, Kano 14.3%). Boxplots compare distributions but may be unfamiliar to non-technical audiences; a bar chart or line plot can often make the same point more directly.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb2",
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb3"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f1",
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f2",
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f3",
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f4",
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f5"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.r02.b2",
              "role": "misconception",
              "text": "Comparing pie slices looks easy, but it is not as easy as comparing bar lengths. A bar chart's common baseline makes differences immediately readable; two similar-sized pie slices are hard to rank correctly by eye. When precise comparison between categories matters, a bar chart is the stronger choice.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb5"
                ],
